# .gitattributes
# Purpose: stop "phantom" diffs and fake merge conflicts caused by Windows and
# macOS/Linux disagreeing about line endings (CRLF vs LF).
#
# Background: Windows editors tend to save files with CRLF line endings, while
# macOS/Linux use LF. Without this file, the same unchanged line can show up as
# a change just because someone opened it on a different OS. These rules pin the
# behavior so everyone gets consistent, clean diffs.

# --- Default for every file ---
# "text=auto" lets Git detect which files are text and normalize their line
# endings. "eol=lf" means: store LF in the repo, so history stays consistent no
# matter who committed. (Git still checks files out in a working form your OS is
# happy with.)
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Shell scripts must always be LF ---
# Bash scripts break if they contain CRLF (you get "bad interpreter" errors), so
# force LF on them explicitly.
*.sh text eol=lf

# --- PowerShell scripts ---
# These run on Windows. Treat them as text and normalize them too; PowerShell is
# fine with LF, and this keeps diffs clean.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# --- Binary / image files ---
# These are NOT text. Marking them "binary" tells Git never to touch line
# endings or try to merge them line-by-line (which would corrupt them).
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary

# --- SVG is text (it's XML) ---
# SVGs are actually text, so we let them normalize like other text files. If you
# ever hit odd diffs on an SVG, you can change this line to "*.svg binary".
*.svg text eol=lf
